What the code is doing (statistical explanation)

Load and prepare data
The code imports weekly COVID-19 indicator values for six Idaho counties. It removes the most recent week (likely because data may be incomplete) and calculates a simple regional average.

Use historical baselines for each county
For every county, the script loads a baseline mean and standard deviation (SD).
These values act as the expected normal activity level based on historical data.

Apply Exponentially Weighted Moving Average (EWMA)
For each county, an EWMA time series is calculated.

EWMA smooths the raw weekly data.

It gives more weight to recent weeks while still incorporating past values.

This is a common outbreak-detection method because it highlights sustained increases in activity, not just single-week spikes.
The smoothing parameter, α = 0.3, determines how quickly the algorithm responds to recent changes.

Convert EWMA values into statistical “signal” thresholds
For each county, the code calculates several thresholds:

2 SD, 4 SD, 7 SD, 9 SD, 10 SD above baseline
These thresholds represent increasing levels of activity, with higher SDs meaning stronger signals of abnormal disease transmission.

Assign Activity Levels (1–5) based on deviation from baseline
Using the threshold system, each county’s EWMA value is classified into an activity category:

1 = within 2 SD → baseline/low activity

2 = 3–4 SD above → moderate increase

3 = 5–7 SD above → high activity

4 = 8–9 SD above → very high activity

5 = ≥10 SD above → extremely high / strong outbreak signal

This transforms continuous EWMA signals into an easy-to-interpret categorical alert level.

Output the most recent activity classification
The script extracts the latest week (after EWMA smoothing) and provides the activity category for each county.
It then reshapes these results into long format for visualization or reporting.

In short

The code:

(1) smooths weekly COVID activity using EWMA,
(2) compares activity to county-specific historical baselines,
(3) calculates how many SDs each county is above expected levels, and
(4) converts those results into a 5-level activity classification system.

This is essentially a statistical aberration-detection approach commonly used in syndromic surveillance and outbreak analytics.
